{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 894 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9ee8ea412aefbf5c79790dc359fdad1a44cf53f6df365c19220b144bd7125844" :edb/db-before-t 893 :edb/db-after-t 894 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534206 50 #inst "2026-10-06T10:46:04.549Z" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157068 1100 "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157068 1100 "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 > 0$." 13194139534206 false] [17592186157068 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157068 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4e^{x} - 5 & > 0 \\\\ 4e^{x} & > 5 \\\\ e^{x} & > \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln (e^{x}) & > \\ln (\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & > \\ln 5 - \\ln 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534206 false] [17592186157069 1100 "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157069 1100 "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x < 7^{3-x}$." 13194139534206 false] [17592186157069 1145 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{x} & <{7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157069 1145 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 5^{x} & < 7^{3 - x} \\\\ \\ln (3 \\cdot 5^{x}) & < \\ln (7^{3 - x}) \\\\ \\ln 3 + \\ln (5^{x}) & < \\ln (7^{3 - x}) \\\\ \\ln 3 + x\\ln 5 & < (3 - x)\\ln 7 \\\\ \\ln 3 + x\\ln 5 & < 3\\ln 7 - x\\ln 7 \\\\ x\\ln 5 + x\\ln 7 & < 3\\ln 7 - \\ln 3 \\\\ x(\\ln 5 + \\ln 7) & < 3\\ln 7 - \\ln 3 \\\\ x & < \\frac{3\\ln 7 - \\ln 3}{\\ln 5 + \\ln 7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139534206 false] [17592186157075 1100 "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157075 1100 "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12$." 13194139534206 false] [17592186157075 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157075 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} + 2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ (\\frac{3}{4})^{x} & \\ge 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln [(\\frac{3}{4})^{x}] & \\ge \\ln 2 \\\\ x\\ln (\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\ln 2 \\\\ x(\\ln 3 - \\ln 4) & \\ge \\ln 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\nx \\mid \\le \\frac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 3 - \\ln 4}\n$$" 13194139534206 false] [17592186157076 1100 "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0$." 13194139534206 false] [17592186157076 1100 "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0.$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157076 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{8-x}+12 & >0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {3}^{8-x} & >-12 \\\\ {3}^{8-x} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" 13194139534206 true] [17592186157076 1145 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\cdot 3^{8 - x} + 12 & > 0 \\\\ 4 \\cdot 3^{8 - x} & > - 12 \\\\ 3^{8 - x} & > - 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0$, we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x$.\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x$." 13194139534206 false] [17592186157360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282197 13194139534206 true] [17592186157360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282246 13194139534206 true] [17592186157360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282251 13194139534206 true] [17592186157360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282257 13194139534206 true] [17592186157360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282262 13194139534206 true] [175921861573